---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { techArticleSchema, gitDates } from '../../lib/techarticle.js';

const techArticleJsonLd = techArticleSchema({
  sourcePath: 'src/pages/careers/salary.astro',
  path: '/careers/salary',
  headline: 'Oil Rig Salaries 2026: $38k to $300k by Role',
  description: 'Real 2026 pay for every rig role — roustabout $38k to company man $300k+ — plus onshore vs offshore differences and the certs each step needs.',
  lang: 'en',
});

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What is the highest-paying job on an oil rig?',
    a: "Company man, at $150,000 to $300,000-plus a year — he's the operator's own representative on site and holds final authority over the well program. OIM/rig manager ($150,000–$280,000+) and toolpusher ($90,000–$180,000) are the highest-paying drilling-contractor roles, since both carry whole-rig operational responsibility rather than running a single crew.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you need experience to start working on an oil rig?',
    a: 'No. Roustabout is the standard entry point and requires no prior rig experience — just physical fitness and a willingness to learn. It pays the least on the rig ($38,000–$60,000) precisely because it carries no certification requirement and no specialized skill yet.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much more does offshore pay than onshore?',
    a: "About 30–40% more for the same role, plus free room and board during the hitch and an extended rotation off (commonly 14/14 or 28/28) — see the offshore vs onshore comparison above for the full roughneck example.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Is oil rig pay the same as the hourly rate advertised in a job posting?',
    a: "Not directly. Most postings advertise an hourly rate against a 12-hour tour, and the actual annual total depends on the rotation schedule (a 14/14 pattern and a 21/21 pattern at the same hourly rate produce very different yearly totals). Some roles also carry a separate per diem on top of wages. Always compare annualized totals, not hourly rates alone.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Why do two people in the same job make different pay?',
    a: "Region and basin activity, day-rate contract work versus staff employment with benefits, and where the commodity cycle sits all move the number more than the job title does — see Reading the Numbers below for the full breakdown.",
  },
];

const lastUpdated = gitDates('src/pages/careers/salary.astro').dateModified;
const lastUpdatedLabel = lastUpdated
  ? new Date(lastUpdated).toLocaleDateString('en-US', { year: 'numeric', month: 'long', day: 'numeric' })
  : null;
---
<BaseLayout
  {/* Title and description are length-budgeted deliberately. BaseLayout appends
      " | RigFloorHQ" (13 chars), and the previous title was 58 before that, so
      Google truncated it mid-phrase and the brand never showed. This one is 44,
      landing at 57 with the suffix.

      Leading with the real range from the table below is the differentiator:
      this page competes at position ~6 against Indeed, Glassdoor and
      ZipRecruiter, whose listings look identical to each other in the SERP.
      A concrete $38k–$300k+ span is the one thing they do not show up front.
      Keep these numbers in sync with the pay table if it is ever updated. */}
  title="Oil Rig Salaries 2026: $38k to $300k by Role"
  description="Real 2026 pay for every rig role — roustabout $38k to company man $300k+ — plus onshore vs offshore differences and the certs each step needs."
  extraJsonLd={[techArticleJsonLd]}
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Careers', href: '/careers' }, { label: 'Salary Guide', href: '/careers/salary' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/careers/salary"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/careers/salary" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">Careers — Compensation</div>
    <h1>Oil Rig <span>Salary Guide</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">What every position actually pays in 2026, and why offshore consistently pays more than the equivalent onshore role.</p>
    {lastUpdatedLabel && <p style="color:var(--fog);font-size:13px;margin-top:8px;">Updated {lastUpdatedLabel}</p>}
  </div>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="note-box" style="max-width:800px;">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Oil rig pay ranges from about $38,000 a year for an entry-level roustabout to $300,000-plus for a company man, with most experienced hands clearing $70,000 to $120,000. Offshore roles pay 30–40% more than the same job onshore, and BLS data independently puts toolpushers at $124,100 a year nationally.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">By Position</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">2026 Pay <span>Ranges</span></h2>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Position</th><th>Entry / Low</th><th>Typical</th><th>Senior / High</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Roustabout</td><td>$38,000</td><td>$50,000</td><td>$60,000</td><td>No experience required</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Roughneck / Floorhand</td><td>$45,000</td><td>$70,000</td><td>$82,000+</td><td>1–3 yrs experience typical</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Derrickhand</td><td>$55,000</td><td>$75,000</td><td>$90,000</td><td>Works at height, mud system responsibility</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Assistant Driller</td><td>$60,000</td><td>$78,000</td><td>$95,000</td><td>IWCF Level 2 required</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Driller</td><td>$70,000</td><td>$95,000</td><td>$120,000</td><td>IWCF Level 3 / Driller required</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Toolpusher</td><td>$90,000</td><td>$130,000</td><td>$180,000</td><td>Manages entire rig operation. See <a href="/careers/toolpusher" style="color:var(--amber);">toolpusher guide</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>OIM / Rig Manager</td><td>$150,000</td><td>$210,000</td><td>$280,000+</td><td>Top operational role on installation. See <a href="/careers/oim-rig-manager" style="color:var(--amber);">OIM / rig manager guide</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mud Engineer</td><td>$80,000</td><td>$105,000</td><td>$140,000</td><td>Employed by service company</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Directional Driller</td><td>$100,000</td><td>$150,000</td><td>$200,000+</td><td>MWD/LWD specialization</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wellsite Geologist</td><td>$80,000</td><td>$115,000</td><td>$160,000</td><td>Geology degree required</td></tr>
          <tr><td>HSE / Safety Officer</td><td>$65,000</td><td>$85,000</td><td>$110,000</td><td>NEBOSH or OSHA 30</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rig Welder (Junior)</td><td>$45,000</td><td>$55,000</td><td>$65,000</td><td>See <a href="/welding" style="color:var(--amber);">full welding guide</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rig Welder (Senior)</td><td>$90,000</td><td>$110,000</td><td>$130,000</td><td>All-position pipe welding</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Underwater Welder</td><td>$60,000</td><td>$100,000</td><td>$150,000+</td><td>Plus depth/saturation pay. See <a href="/welding/underwater-welding" style="color:var(--amber);">hyperbaric welding</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Company Man</td><td>$150,000</td><td>$220,000</td><td>$300,000+</td><td>Operator's representative. See <a href="/careers/company-man" style="color:var(--amber);">company man guide</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="note-box">
      <strong>Note on figures:</strong> This table is RigFloorHQ's own editorial estimate — compiled from job postings, recruiter listings, and industry pay discussion through 2025–2026, not a single named survey. It's not independently sourced the way the government table below is, which is exactly why that table exists: use it as the real, citable cross-check against this one. Actual pay varies significantly by region, employer, rig type, and market conditions. Rates in the Gulf of Mexico, North Sea, and Middle East tend to sit at the higher end; land rig rates in slower basins sit lower.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="bls-wage-data">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Government Data</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Official BLS Wages, <span>by Role and State</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:760px;margin-bottom:20px;">The table above is an industry-compiled estimate. For an independently verifiable cross-check, here's what the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics' own Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS) survey reports for core oil and gas extraction roles — real government wage data, not a third-party aggregate.</p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>BLS Occupation</th><th>National Annual Mean Wage</th><th>Closest Role on This Page</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Roustabouts</td><td>$49,040</td><td>Roustabout</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Rotary Drill Operators</td><td>$73,780</td><td>Driller</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Derrick Operators</td><td>$74,890</td><td>Derrickhand</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Service Unit Operators</td><td>$72,740</td><td>Assistant Driller</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wellhead Pumpers</td><td>$74,000</td><td>Production/field operator</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Petroleum Engineers</td><td>$169,280</td><td>Directional Driller / engineering-track roles</td></tr>
          <tr><td>First-Line Supervisors of Construction Trades and Extraction Workers</td><td>$124,100</td><td>Toolpusher</td></tr>
          <tr><td>General and Operations Managers</td><td>$194,910</td><td>OIM / Rig Manager</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);font-size:13px;max-width:760px;margin-top:10px;">Source: <a href="https://www.bls.gov/opub/mlr/2026/article/occupational-employment-and-wages-in-oil-and-gas-industries-may-2024.htm" style="color:var(--amber);">BLS Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics, May 2024, Monthly Labor Review</a>. The oil and gas extraction industry as a whole had a $114,750 annual mean wage across all occupations in this survey — the BLS's own highest-paying segment of the broader oil and gas industry.</p>

    <h3 style="font-family:var(--cond);font-weight:700;font-size:18px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);letter-spacing:0.04em;margin:28px 0 14px;">Where It Pays Most: Derrick Operators by State</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>State</th><th>Annual Mean Wage</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Alaska</td><td>$76,070</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pennsylvania</td><td>$75,280</td></tr>
          <tr><td>North Dakota</td><td>$67,850</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wyoming</td><td>$61,510</td></tr>
          <tr><td>New Mexico</td><td>$59,020</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);font-size:13px;max-width:760px;margin-top:10px;">Source: <a href="https://www.bls.gov/oes/" style="color:var(--amber);">BLS Occupational Employment and Wage Statistics (OEWS)</a>, May 2023 — the most recent survey with a published state-by-state breakdown for this occupation at time of writing. These are statewide averages across every employer in the state, so a specific operator or rig will differ; this is a sample of the real spread behind the $58,330 national mean, not a literal floor and ceiling — other states fall both above and below this range. The states with the most rigs running aren't the same as the states that pay the most: Texas ($57,400) and Oklahoma ($55,310) sit below every state in the table above despite far higher rig counts, since their totals include a much larger mix of smaller, lower-activity operations alongside their highest-intensity basins.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="offshore-vs-onshore">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Comparison</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Offshore vs <span>Onshore</span></h2>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Offshore roles pay <strong>30–40% more</strong> than the equivalent onshore position. The premium compensates for several real tradeoffs:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Isolation:</strong> You're living on the installation for the full rotation, away from family and normal life.</li>
          <li><strong>Rotation schedule:</strong> Common patterns are 14 days on / 14 off, or 28/28 for deepwater. You work long stretches, then have extended time off — a real lifestyle tradeoff, not just a pay difference.</li>
          <li><strong>Risk profile:</strong> Helicopter transport, marine conditions, and confined-space work environments carry different risks than land operations.</li>
          <li><strong>Physical conditions:</strong> No leaving site during your hitch. Weather, sea state, and confined living quarters for weeks at a time.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>In exchange, offshore workers typically get free room and board during the hitch (meaning most of your pay is disposable income), plus the extended time off that a 14/14 or 28/28 rotation provides — effectively 26 weeks of the year free on a 28/28 schedule.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 style="font-family:var(--cond);font-weight:700;font-size:18px;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--white);letter-spacing:0.04em;margin-bottom:16px;">Example: Roughneck Pay Comparison</h3>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1px;background:var(--iron);border:1px solid var(--iron);">
          <div style="background:var(--steel);padding:18px 20px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;">
            <span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);">Onshore Roughneck</span>
            <span class="mono" style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--white);">$70,000/yr</span>
          </div>
          <div style="background:var(--steel);padding:18px 20px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;">
            <span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--fog);">Offshore Roughneck</span>
            <span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--amber);">$91,000–$98,000/yr</span>
          </div>
          <div style="background:var(--iron);padding:18px 20px;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;">
            <span style="font-size:13px;color:var(--mist);font-weight:600;">Premium</span>
            <span style="font-family:var(--mono);color:var(--safe);">+30–40%</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="note-box" style="margin-top:24px;">
          <strong>Which is right for you?</strong> If you have family obligations at home and prefer daily routine, onshore work with predictable local schedules may suit you better. If you can handle extended time away and want to maximize savings, offshore's higher pay plus extended blocks of time off is hard to beat financially.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block" id="hourly-per-diem">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Pay Structure</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Hourly Rates <span>and Per Diem</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:760px;margin-bottom:20px;">Most of the figures above are annual totals, but rig pay is actually built from an hourly rate against a 12-hour tour, plus — for some roles — a separate daily allowance. Converting annual pay to an hourly figure using a standard 2,080-hour full-time-equivalent year gives a rough, comparable baseline, but it is not the actual contract rate: real tours and rotations run more or fewer hours than that baseline, which is exactly why the annual total, not the hourly number alone, is what actually lands in the bank.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Typical Annual</th><th>≈ Hourly (÷2,080 hrs)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Roustabout</td><td>$50,000</td><td>≈ $24/hr</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Roughneck / Floorhand</td><td>$70,000</td><td>≈ $34/hr</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Derrickhand</td><td>$75,000</td><td>≈ $36/hr</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Driller</td><td>$95,000</td><td>≈ $46/hr</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:760px;margin-top:16px;">Many land-based contractors also pay a <strong>per diem</strong> — a fixed daily allowance, typically in the $75–$150 range, covering meals and lodging while away from home — on top of wages, separate from the hourly rate and usually non-taxable. Offshore roles generally don't carry a per diem at all, because room and board on the installation is already provided free for the full hitch, which is part of why the headline offshore pay premium in the comparison above understates the real gap in disposable income.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">Reading the Numbers</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Why Two Hands in the Same Role <span>Earn Differently</span></h2>
    <p style="color:var(--fog);max-width:740px;margin-bottom:24px;">Published ranges hide most of what determines an actual payslip. Four factors move the figure more than job title does.</p>

    <div class="card-grid grid-2">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Hours, not rate</h3>
        <p>Most rig pay is hourly against a 12-hour tour, so gross earnings track days worked as much as rate. A 14/14 rotation and a 21/21 rotation at identical hourly rates produce very different annual totals. Compare annualised figures, never hourly rates alone.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Day rate vs staff</h3>
        <p>Contract and agency hands often show a higher headline rate that has to cover unpaid time between hitches, insurance and pension. A lower staff rate with paid leave and benefits can be worth more over a year. They are not comparable numbers as published.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Region and basin</h3>
        <p>Rates track local activity and cost of living, and move with the rig count. The same role in an active basin during a boom and a quiet one in a downturn can differ by a wide margin — which is why any published range is a snapshot, not a rate card.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Commodity cycle</h3>
        <p>This industry is cyclical, and pay follows drilling activity with a lag. Ranges compiled in an upswing overstate what is available in a downturn. Treat every figure on this page, and anywhere else, as period-specific.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="note-box mt-24" style="max-width:800px;">
      <strong>What actually raises your rate.</strong> Certifications you hold before they are required, a clean safety record, and continuity with one contractor. Rig-hopping for a small rate bump repeatedly resets the trust that drives promotion — the trade-off is covered in <a href="/blog/roustabout-to-driller-timeline" style="color:var(--amber);">roustabout to driller</a>. Which tickets pay for themselves is on the <a href="/careers/certifications" style="color:var(--amber);">certifications page</a>, and how the pay steps from driller to toolpusher to OIM map onto the supervisory career ladder is in the <a href="/pillars/rig-floor-leadership" style="color:var(--amber);">rig floor leadership guide</a>.
    </div>

    <p style="color:var(--fog);font-size:13px;max-width:800px;margin-top:20px;">These figures are indicative ranges compiled for orientation, not offers, quotes or a guide to what any specific employer pays. Verify against current job postings and your own contract before making a financial decision.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="section-eyebrow">FAQ</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Frequently Asked <span>Questions</span></h2>
    <div class="accordion">
      {faq.map((item) => (
        <details class="acc-item">
          <summary class="acc-header">{item.q}<span class="acc-arrow">▼</span></summary>
          <div class="acc-body"><p>{item.a}</p></div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
